IMPORT prelude

-- Income Tax Assessment Act 1997 (Commonwealth of Australia) -- how income tax is worked out (ss 4-10, 4-15), ordinary income and
-- general deductions (ss 6-5, 8-1), the Low Income tax offset (ss 61-110, 61-115), the working Australians tax offset
-- (ss 61-155, 61-160), tax losses (ss 36-10, 36-17), and deductible gifts (s 30-15, with the limit in s 26-55).
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 267, compilation date 27 August 2026 (includes amendments up to Act No. 79, 2026; register id
-- C2026C00400, 12 volumes), as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/C2004A05138.txt.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0001 in subjects/au/requirements.jsonl (priority 1): the Act is raised 24 times by the three
-- Western Australian cradle-to-grave lives (subjects/au/scenarios/wa-cradle-to-grave-{chloe,jake,daniel}). SCOPED to the
-- provisions those lives ask about and the definitions, conditions and exceptions needed to answer them. This module answers
-- chloe c045 and c048 (first returns), c083 (self-education), jake k043 (a sole trader's return), daniel d04a (a company's
-- loss), d072 (a $2,000,000 gift to a private ancillary fund) and the testamentary-gift part of d093. The other modules of this
-- encoding are itaa-business.l4 (Div 83A, Div 355, Subdiv 360-A), itaa-cgt.l4 (Divs 115, 118, 128, 152) and itaa-super.l4
-- (Divs 290, 292, 294, 301, 302).
--
-- NOT ENCODED. The rates themselves: basic income tax liability comes from the Income Tax Rates Act 1986 (s 4-10 Step 2), so it is
-- an INPUT here, as is the tax-free threshold the working Australians offset refers to. The Medicare levy and HELP repayments
-- (other Acts). What counts as ordinary income or a business (case law). The trustee limbs of Div 61, the under-18 cap
-- (s 61-115(2)), net exempt income beyond a single input, the continuity-of-ownership and business-continuity tests for company
-- losses (Subdiv 165-A, 165-B), the 30-B tables of named recipients, gifts of property, and the spreading of gift deductions
-- (Subdiv 30-DB). The application date of the working Australians tax offset was not found in the section and is not modelled.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. NOT FOR PUBLIC USE. See NOTES.md.

§ `Income Tax Assessment Act 1997 -- working out income tax`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser of` MEANS
    IF `a` AT MOST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the greater of` MEANS
    IF `a` AT LEAST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

§§ `4-15 -- taxable income`

-- s 4-15(1) Step 3: "Subtract your deductions from your assessable income (unless they exceed it). The result is your taxable
-- income. (If the deductions equal or exceed the assessable income, you don't have a taxable income.)"
-- Nil stands for "no taxable income".
GIVEN `assessable income` IS A NUMBER, `deductions` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the taxable income` MEANS
    `the greater of` (`assessable income` MINUS `deductions`) 0

§§ `4-10 -- income tax for the financial year`

-- s 4-10(3): Step 2 "Work out your basic income tax liability on your taxable income using ... the income tax rate or rates
-- that apply to you" (the Income Tax Rates Act 1986 -- an INPUT here); Step 3 work out your tax offsets; Step 4 "Subtract your
-- *tax offsets from your basic income tax liability. The result is how much income tax you owe".
-- s 63-10 table item 20: "Any *tax offset not covered by another item" -- "You cannot get a refund of it, you cannot transfer it
-- and you cannot carry it forward". The Low Income and working Australians offsets are such offsets (item 3 names the working
-- Australians offset with the same result), so they cannot take the tax owed below nil.
GIVEN `basic income tax liability` IS A NUMBER, `non-refundable offsets` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the income tax owed` MEANS
    `the greater of` (`basic income tax liability` MINUS `non-refundable offsets`) 0

§§ `61-110, 61-115 -- Low Income tax offset`

-- s 61-110(1): entitled for 2020-21 or a later income year if "(a) you are an individual who is an Australian resident at any
-- time during the income year; and (b) your taxable income for the income year does not exceed $66,667."
-- s 61-115(1) table: "does not exceed $37,500 -- $700"; "exceeds $37,500 but is not more than $45,000 -- $700, less an amount
-- equal to 5% of the excess"; "exceeds $45,000 but is not more than $66,667 -- $325, less an amount equal to 1.5% of the excess".
GIVEN `resident at any time in the year` IS A BOOLEAN, `taxable income` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the Low Income tax offset` MEANS
    IF (NOT `resident at any time in the year`) OR `taxable income` GREATER THAN 66667 THEN 0
    ELSE IF `taxable income` AT MOST 37500 THEN 700
         ELSE IF `taxable income` AT MOST 45000 THEN 700 MINUS (`taxable income` MINUS 37500) TIMES 5 DIVIDED BY 100
              ELSE `the greater of` (325 MINUS (`taxable income` MINUS 45000) TIMES 15 DIVIDED BY 1000) 0

§§ `61-155, 61-160 -- working Australians tax offset`

-- s 61-155(1): entitled if an Australian resident at any time in the year and "the amount worked out for you under subsection (2)
-- (about your net labour income) exceeds the tax-free threshold (within the meaning of the Income Tax Rates Act 1986)".
-- s 61-160: "the lesser of: (a) $250; and (b) the amount that would be the amount of your basic income tax liability ... if your
-- taxable income was comprised only of your net labour income". The threshold and that notional liability are INPUTS.
GIVEN `resident at any time in the year` IS A BOOLEAN, `net labour income` IS A NUMBER, `tax-free threshold` IS A NUMBER,
      `basic liability on net labour income alone` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the working Australians tax offset` MEANS
    IF `resident at any time in the year` AND `net labour income` GREATER THAN `tax-free threshold`
    THEN `the lesser of` 250 `basic liability on net labour income alone`
    ELSE 0

§§ `6-5, 8-1 -- ordinary income and general deductions`

-- s 6-5(2): a resident's assessable income includes ordinary income "derived directly or indirectly from all sources, whether in
-- or out of Australia"; (3) a foreign resident's includes ordinary income from "all *Australian sources".
GIVEN `Australian resident` IS A BOOLEAN, `from an Australian source` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the ordinary income is assessable` IF
    `Australian resident` OR `from an Australian source`

-- s 8-1(1): deductible "to the extent that (a) it is incurred in gaining or producing your assessable income; or (b) it is
-- necessarily incurred in carrying on a *business"; (2) not "to the extent that (a) it is a loss or outgoing of capital, or of a
-- capital nature; or (b) ... of a private or domestic nature; or (c) ... incurred in relation to gaining or producing your
-- *exempt income or your *non-assessable non-exempt income; or (d) a provision of this Act prevents you from deducting it."
-- Whether a course fee is incurred in gaining current income (c083) is a question of fact and case law, so it is an input.
DECLARE Outgoing HAS
    `incurred in gaining or producing assessable income` IS A BOOLEAN
    `necessarily incurred in carrying on a business` IS A BOOLEAN
    `capital or of a capital nature` IS A BOOLEAN
    `private or domestic` IS A BOOLEAN
    `relates to exempt or non-assessable non-exempt income` IS A BOOLEAN
    `another provision prevents the deduction` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the outgoing` IS AN Outgoing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the outgoing is a general deduction` IF
        (`the outgoing`'s `incurred in gaining or producing assessable income` OR `the outgoing`'s `necessarily incurred in carrying on a business`)
    AND (NOT `the outgoing`'s `capital or of a capital nature`)
    AND (NOT `the outgoing`'s `private or domestic`)
    AND (NOT `the outgoing`'s `relates to exempt or non-assessable non-exempt income`)
    AND (NOT `the outgoing`'s `another provision prevents the deduction`)

§§ `36-10, 36-17 -- tax losses`

-- s 36-10: add up the deductions (except earlier tax losses), "(2) Subtract your total assessable income. (3) If you *derived
-- *exempt income, also subtract your *net exempt income ... (4) Any amount remaining is your tax loss".
GIVEN `deductions` IS A NUMBER, `assessable income` IS A NUMBER, `net exempt income` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the tax loss` MEANS
    `the greater of` (`deductions` MINUS `assessable income` MINUS `net exempt income`) 0

-- s 36-17(2), a corporate tax entity with no net exempt income: "If the entity's total assessable income for the later income
-- year exceeds the entity's total deductions (except *tax losses), the entity is to deduct from that excess so much of the tax
-- loss as the entity chooses. The entity may choose a nil amount." (Contrast s 36-15(2): an individual "deduct[s] the tax loss
-- from that excess" -- no choice.)
GIVEN `later year assessable income` IS A NUMBER, `later year deductions` IS A NUMBER, `undeducted loss` IS A NUMBER,
      `amount chosen` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the company's loss deduction` MEANS
    `the lesser of` (`the lesser of` `amount chosen` `undeducted loss`)
                    (`the greater of` (`later year assessable income` MINUS `later year deductions`) 0)

GIVEN `later year assessable income` IS A NUMBER, `later year deductions` IS A NUMBER, `undeducted loss` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the individual's loss deduction` MEANS
    `the lesser of` `undeducted loss` (`the greater of` (`later year assessable income` MINUS `later year deductions`) 0)

§§ `30-15, 26-55 -- deductible gifts`

-- s 30-15(1) table item 1: a gift of money to "A fund, authority or institution covered by an item in any of the tables in
-- Subdivision 30-B" -- you can deduct "the amount you are giving"; special conditions: "(a) the fund, authority or institution
-- must be in Australia; and (aa) ... must either meet the requirements of section 30-17 or be mentioned by name". Item 2 makes
-- the same provision for an *ancillary fund (a private ancillary fund, as in d072).
-- s 30-15(2): "A testamentary gift or contribution is not deductible under this section."
-- s 26-55: the total of Division 30 deductions is limited to assessable income less all other deductions (except tax losses).
-- Recipient status (endorsed or named, in Australia, s 30-17) is collapsed into one input.
GIVEN `money given` IS A NUMBER, `recipient qualifies under item 1 or 2` IS A BOOLEAN, `testamentary` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the gift deduction before the limit` MEANS
    IF `recipient qualifies under item 1 or 2` AND NOT `testamentary` THEN `money given` ELSE 0

GIVEN `money given` IS A NUMBER, `recipient qualifies under item 1 or 2` IS A BOOLEAN, `testamentary` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `assessable income` IS A NUMBER, `other deductions` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the gift deduction for the year` MEANS
    `the lesser of` (`the gift deduction before the limit` `money given` `recipient qualifies under item 1 or 2` `testamentary`)
                    (`the greater of` (`assessable income` MINUS `other deductions`) 0)
